==English==
{{wp|Innocence (disambiguation)}}
{{wikiquote}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*neḱ-}}
{{inh+|en|enm}}, from {{der|en|fro|inocence}}, from {{der|en|la|innocentia}}. Displaced native {{noncog|ang|unsċyld}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪnəsn̩s/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-innocence.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[absence|Absence]] of [[responsibility]] for a [[crime]], [[tort]], etc.
#: {{syn|en|unguilt}}
#: {{ant|en|guilt}}
#: {{ux|en|Her attorney managed to convince the jury of her '''innocence'''.}}
# [[lack|Lack]] of [[understanding]] about sensitive subjects such as sexuality and crime.
#: {{syn|en|naivety}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=9|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Eustace gaped at him in amazement. When his urbanity dropped away from him, as now, he had an '''innocence''' of expression which was almost infantile. It was as if the world had never touched him at all.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
#: {{ux|en|In his '''innocence''', he offered the stranger to bring the package to Paris, never suspecting it contained drugs.}}
# [[lack|Lack]] of [[ability]] or [[intention]] to [[harm]] or damage.
#: {{syn|en|harmlessness}}
#: {{ant|en|harmfulness}}
#: {{ux|en|Tests have demonstrated the '''innocence''' of this substance.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1967
|author=w:Barbara Sleigh
|title={{w|Jessamy}}
|year_published=1993
|location=Sevenoaks, Kent
|publisher=Bloomsbury
|isbn=0 340 19547 9
|page=96
|url=
|passage=His unruly hair was slicked down with water, and as Jessamy introduced him to Miss Brindle his face assumed a cherubic '''innocence''' which would immediately have aroused the suspicions of anyone who knew him.
}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[imbecility|Imbecility]]; [[mental]] [[deficiency]].

